1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Kohen Ṣedeq Bar Ivomay Gaon
2 CONT
2 CONT Kohen Ṣedeq Bar Ivomay (or Ikhomay) Gaon was head of the Sura yeshiva in Iraq from 838 to 848 (alternatively, 832 to 843). From the few sources attributable to him with a measure of confidence, it appears that Kohen Ṣedeq was one 
2 CONC of the Babylonian authorities consulted on legal matters by the heads of the Jewish community of Qayrawan. A ruling of his on a liturgical question is also cited by the Andalusian scholar Isaac ben Judah ibn Ghiyyāth, and the refe
2 CONC rence therein to “the seder of Rav Kohen Ṣedeq” has led to the suggestion that he was one of the first to compile a prayerbook. Another of his responsa, concerning circumcision, reveals a respectful attitude toward local custom ev
2 CONC en when it diverged from the practice of the Babylonian academies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnold Franklin
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Abramson, Shraga. Ba-Merkazim uva-Tefuṣot bi-Tequfat ha-Ge’onim (Jerusalem: Mosad Harav Kook, 1965).
2 CONT
2 CONT Gil, Moshe. The Jews in Islamic Countries in the Middle Ages (Leiden: Brill, 2004).
2 CONT
2 CONT Havatzelet, Me’ir. Ha-Rambam veha-Ge’onim (Jerusalem and New York: Sura, 1967).
2 CONT
2 CONT Tykocinski, Hayyim. Taqqanot ha-Ge’onim, trans. and ed. Me’ir Havatzelet (Jerusalem and New York: Sura and Yeshiva University, 1959).
2 CONT
