1 NAME Hārūn 'Aaron' /ibn ʿImrān 'Me'ir'/ Radhani, Jahābidha al-ḥadra [Abassid Banker]
2 GIVN Hārūn 'Aaron'
2 NSFX Radhani, Jahābidha al-ḥadra [Abassid Banker]
2 _MARNM ibn ʿImrān 'Me'ir'
2 NICK Hārūn ibn ʿImrān
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 901
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 928
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Aaron ben Amram—or, as his name is given in Arabic sources, Hārūn ibn ʿImrān—lived in the second half of the ninth century and the first quarter of the tenth. He apparently began his career as a trader, as in one A
2 CONC rabic source he and his partner, Joseph ben Phinehas, are referred to as al-tujjār (the merchants). Eventually he became a jahbadh (banker). In this capacity, with responsibility for administering, remitting, and supplying funds, 
2 CONC Aaron, together with the jahbadh Joseph ben Phinehas (Yūsuf ibn Fīnḥās), played a key role in the financial administration of the ʿAbbasid empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT The banking activities of Aaron and Joseph may already be implied in the decree of Caliph al-Muqtadir in 908/909 restricting Jews to the governmental posts of ṭabīb (physician) and jahbadh (banker)—most likely, as Fischel surmised
2 CONC , to “legalize” the status quo. Under al-Muqtadir, in any event, Aaron and Joseph were officially appointed “bankers of the court” (jahābidha al-ḥadra) by 912/13, as indicated by the joint testimony of the tenth-century Muslim lit
2 CONC térateurs al-Tanūkhī and Hilāl al-Ṣābiʾ. Aaron and Joseph were not just two among a larger number of  court bankers, but in fact the two central figures around whom the financial administration of the caliphate revolved, as clearl
2 CONC y evinced by the description of their activities in Arabic sources. Indeed, according to al-Tanūkhī (p. 85), despite the volatile political environment, they retained their official positions until death (when their functions were
2 CONC  taken over by their heirs), for if even one of them had been dismissed, “the business of the Caliph would come to a standstill” (Fischel, p. 28).
2 CONT
2 CONT Aaron died sometime between June 924 and 928, the terminus a quo of which is indicated by the explicit reference to Aaron by the Islamic historian Hilāl al-Ṣābiʾ in connection with the jailing at that time of one of his clients, t
2 CONC he ʿAbbasid vizier  ʿAlī ibn al-Furāt, whereas the terminus ad quem is implied by the eulogism “may the memory of the ingathered be for a blessing” (Heb. zekher ha-neʾesafim li-vrakha) following his name (and that of Mar Neṭīra) i
2 CONC n the patronym “the sons of Aaron” in a letter written that year by Saʿadya Gaon to the Jewish community of Fustat (for the most recently edited text, see Gil, vol. 2, pp. 27–30, esp. p. 30, ll. 3–4).
2 CONT
2 CONT The prominence of Aaron in the eyes of the Jewish community and the implications of his court influence on their behalf are also evident from the high encomium applied to him by Meʾir Gaon in a letter to Babylonian Jewry—the only 
2 CONC Jewish source to mention Aaron directly—“the crown of Israel, our precious and pleasant jewel . . . Aaron ben Amram . . .  savior of the generation, who has not inclined his ear to turn away from the Lord’s precepts” (ʿaṭeret yisr
2 CONC aʾel ʿedyenu  ha-yaqar ve-ha-naʿim . . .  Aharon be-rabbi ʿAmram . . .  moshiaʿ ha-dor asher loʾ hiṭṭa ozno  la-sur meḥuqqe y"y).
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death, Aaron’s functions were taken over by his sons Bishr, Abraham, and ʿAlī, who continued to work together with the heirs of Joseph ben Phinehas—these being the “sons of Aaron” and the “sons of Neṭīra”—i.e., Neṭīra ben
2 CONC  Sahl, Joseph’s son-in-law—mentioned in the letter of Saʿadya cited above.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael G. Wechsler
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT al-Tanūkhī, al-Muḥassin ibn ʿAlī. Kitāb Jāmiʿ al-Tawārīkh: al-Musammā bi-Kitāb Nishwār al-Muḥāḍara wa-Akhbār al-Mudhākara, pt. II, ed. D. S. Margoliouth (Damascus: Maṭbaʻat al-Mufīd, 1930).
2 CONT
2 CONT Fischel, Walter J. Jews in the Economic and Political Life of Mediaeval Islam, 2nd ed. (New York: Ktav, 1969).
2 CONT
2 CONT Gil, Moshe. Be-Malkhut Yishmaʾʿel bi-Tqufat ha-Geʾonim, 4 vols. (Tel Aviv, 1997), vol. 1 trans. D. Strassler, as Jews in Islamic Countries in the Middle Ages (Brill: Leiden, 2004).
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume, A. “Further Documents on the Ben Meir Controversy,” Jewish Quarterly Review, n.s. 5, no. 4 (1915): 543–557.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sassoon, David. A History of the Jews in Baghdad (Letchworth:  D. S. Sassoon, 1949).
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation Michael G. Wechsler. " Aaron ben Amram." Encyclopedia of Jews in the Islamic World. Executive Editor Norman A. Stillman. Brill Online , 2012. Reference. Jim Harlow. 11 July 2012 <http://www.paulyonline.brill.nl/entries/en
2 CONC cyclopedia-of-jews-in-the-islamic-world/aaron-ben-amram-SIM_0000010>
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Aaron ben Me’ir (452 words)
2 CONT
2 CONT Aaron ben Amram—or, as his name is given in Arabic sources, Hārūn ibn ʿImrān—lived in the second half of the ninth century and the first quarter of the tenth. He apparently began his career as a trader, as in one Arabic source he 
2 CONC and his partner,''' Joseph ben Phinehas''', are referred to as al-tujjār (the merchants). Eventually he became a jahbadh (banker). In this capacity, with responsibility for administering, remitting, and supplying funds, Aaron, tog
2 CONC ether with the jahbadh Joseph ben Phinehas (Yūsuf ibn Fīnḥās), played a key role in the financial administration of the ʿAbbasid empire. Aaron ben Amram was a jahbadh (banker) at the Abbasid court during the reign of al-Muqtadir (
2 CONC r. 908–932), a time of fiscal and political crisis in Baghdad.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Abbasid kātib and historian Hilāl al-Ṣābiʾ (d. 1056), the vizier Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī b. Muḥammad Ibn al-Furāt deposited funds confiscated from individuals the regime had executed not with the caliphal fisc but with 
2 CONC '''Joseph ben Phineas''' and another Jewish jahbadh, '''Aaron ben Amram'''—not the first incident of embezzlement in Ibn al-Furāt’s career.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 918, when Ibn al-Furāt was deposed and imprisoned in the palace, the new vizier, ʿAlī b. ʿĪsā, fined the two jahbadhs heavily, but retained their services.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 921, a conflict erupted between the Babylonian yeshivot and the Palestinian yeshiva over the day on which the Passover holiday should be celebrated in 922. According to the Palestinians, the first day of Passover would be a Sun
2 CONC day, whereas the Babylonians held that it would be a Tuesday. The ensuing dispute was but one element in the ongoing struggle in the gaonic pe riod between the  Babylonian yeshivot and the Palestinian yeshiva for hegemony over the
2 CONC  world’s Jewish communities. Both the calendar dispute and the overall conflict ended in a Babylonian victory.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aaron ben Me’ir led the advocates of the Palestinian position, although it is possible that his father,  Me’ir ben Aaron, was still head of the yeshiva at the time. A document fragment from the Cairo Geniza that is ascribed to Sah
2 CONC lān ben Abraham, the leader of the Babylonian community in Fustat, states that Me’ir and his son Aaron served as geonim for fourteen years; according to the calculations of Moshe Gil, this would have been from 912 to 926. The frag
2 CONC ment does not say how the span was divided between father and son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before the outbreak of the calendar conflict, Aaron had supported Mevasser Kahana ben Qimoy against Kohen Ṣedeq for leadership of the Pumbedita yeshiva. He expected Mevasser’s support, in return, in the calendar conflict, but this
2 CONC  hope was shattered when Sa‘adya Gaon (still known as Sa‘adya ben Joseph because he was not yet gaon) managed to unite the leaders of Babylonia against the Palestinians on the calendar issue. Sa‘adya wrote his Sefer ha-Mo‘adim (Bo
2 CONC ok of Seasons) to refute the position taken by Aaron.
2 CONT
2 CONT A faction in Babylonia supported the Palestinians, and apparently a group in the Palestinian yeshiva supported Babylonia. The letter ascribed to Sahlān ben Abraham mentions a Palestinian gaon named Joseph ha-Kohen ben Yoḥay. He ma
2 CONC y have been the leader of Aaron ben Me’ir’s Palestinian opponent. In the Sefer ha-Mo‘adim, Sa‘adya alludes to a conflict between the gaon of Palestine and members of the priestly family. It is possible that the conflict about the 
2 CONC calendar within the Palestinian yeshiva was the factor that led to the yeshiva’s move from Tiberias to Jerusalem.Aaron ben Amram
2 CONT
2 CONT The banking activities of Aaron and Joseph may already be implied in the decree of Caliph al-Muqtadir in 908/909 restricting Jews to the governmental posts of ṭabīb (physician) and jahbadh (banker)—most likely, as Fischel surmised
2 CONC , to “legalize” the status quo. Under al-Muqtadir, in any event, Aaron and Joseph were officially appointed “bankers of the court” (jahābidha al-ḥadra) by 912/13, as indicated by the joint testimony of the tenth-century Muslim lit
2 CONC térateurs al-Tanūkhī and Hilāl al-Ṣābiʾ. Aaron and Joseph were not just two among a larger number of  court bankers, but in fact the two central figures around whom the financial administration of the caliphate revolved, as clearl
2 CONC y evinced by the description of their activities in Arabic sources. Indeed, according to al-Tanūkhī (p. 85), despite the volatile political environment, they retained their official positions until death (when their functions were
2 CONC  taken over by their heirs), for if even one of them had been dismissed, “the business of the Caliph would come to a standstill” (Fischel, p. 28).
2 CONT
2 CONT Aaron died sometime between June 924 and 928, the terminus a quo of which is indicated by the explicit reference to Aaron by the Islamic historian Hilāl al-Ṣābiʾ in connection with the jailing at that time of one of his clients, t
2 CONC he ʿAbbasid vizier  ʿAlī ibn al-Furāt, whereas the terminus ad quem is implied by the eulogism “may the memory of the ingathered be for a blessing” (Heb. zekher ha-neʾesafim li-vrakha) following his name (and that of Mar Neṭīra ) 
2 CONC in the patronym “the sons of Aaron” in a letter written that year by Saʿadya Gaon to the Jewish community of Fustat (for the most recently edited text, see Gil, vol. 2, pp. 27–30, esp. p. 30, ll. 3–4).
2 CONT
2 CONT The prominence of Aaron in the eyes of the Jewish community and the implications of his court influence on their behalf are also evident from the high encomium applied to him by Meʾir Gaon in a letter to Babylonian Jewry—the only 
2 CONC Jewish source to mention Aaron directly—“the crown of Israel, our precious and pleasant jewel . . . Aaron ben Amram . . .  savior of the generation, who has not inclined his ear to turn away from the Lord’s precepts” (ʿaṭeret yisr
2 CONC aʾel ʿedyenu  ha-yaqar ve-ha-naʿim . . .  Aharon be-rabbi ʿAmram . . .  moshiaʿ ha-dor asher loʾ hiṭṭa ozno  la-sur meḥuqqe y"y).
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death, Aaron’s functions were taken over by his sons Bishr, Abraham, and ʿAlī, who continued to work together with the heirs of Joseph ben Phinehas—these being the “sons of Aaron” and the “sons of Neṭīra”—i.e., Neṭīra ben
2 CONC  Sahl, Joseph’s son-in-law—mentioned in the letter of Saʿadya cited above.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael G. Wechsler
2 CONT
