2 CONT  "Sha'are Teshubah," No. 122
2 CONT  Aboab, introduction to "Menorat ha-Ma'or"
2 CONT  Gaon, Sherira (1988). The Iggeres of Rav Sherira Gaon. Translated by Nosson Dovid Rabinowich. Jerusalem: Rabbi Jacob Joseph School Press - Ahavath Torah Institute Moznaim. p. Preface. OCLC 923562173 .
2 CONT  This article incorporates text from a publication now in the public domain: Singer, Isidore; et al., eds. (1901–1906). "SHERIRA B. ḤANINA". The Jewish Encyclopedia. New York: Funk & Wagnalls.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brody, Robert, The Geonim of Babylonia and the Shaping of Medieval Jewish Culture, Yale University Press 1998, repr. 2012: ISBN 978-0300189322 David J. E, “‘As it was Written in the Book of Adam’ The Chronology of the Halakhah and
2 CONC  the Mythical Perception of History in the Late Pumbeditian Thought” Tarbitz, 74:2 (2006).(Heb.) External links Lecture on Rav Sherira Gaon
2 CONT  by Dr. Henry Abramson Lecture, Henry Abramson: Letter of Rav Sherira Gaon (First Person Accounts in Jewish History)
2 CONT  on YouTube, Jan 2020. Iggeret Rav Sherira Gaon , Da'at Encyclopedia (in Hebrew) https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherira_Gaon
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT source: http://www.geni.com/people/Sherira-ben-Hananya-Gaon-of-Pumbeditha/4742052302620080262?through=4127288428870050022
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherira_Gaon
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/vl/shrira/shrira01.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.jewishgen.org/rabbinic/journal/descent_part2.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.chabad.org/library/article_cdo/aid/112507/jewish/Rav-Sherira-Gaon.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://cojs.org/cojswiki/Iggeret_Rav_Sherira_Gaon_1-2:_The_First_Generation_of_the_Tannaim
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Rav Sherira Gaon (Hebrew: רב שרירא גאון) was the head of the yeshiva in Pumbeditha. He wrote the Iggeret Rav Sherira Gaon ("[The] Epistle of Rav Sherira Gaon"), a comprehensive history of the composition of the Talmud. One of the 
2 CONC most prominent Geonim of his period, he was the father of Hai Gaon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaon of Pumbedita; born about 900; died about 1000 (Abraham ibn Daud, "Sefer ha-Ḳabbalah," in Neubauer, "M. J. C." i. 66-67).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the descendant, both on his father's and his mother's side, of prominent families, several members of which had occupied the gaonate.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of his ancestors was Rabbah b. Abuha, who himself belonged to the family of the exilarch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sherira boasted that his genealogy could be traced back to the pre-Bostanaian branch of that family, which, he claimed, on account of the deterioration of the exilarchate had renounced its claims thereto, preferring instead the sc
2 CONC holar's life (Letter of Sherira Gaon, in Neubauer, l.c. i. 23, 33).
2 CONT
2 CONT The seal of his family was a lion, which was said to have been the emblem of the Judean kings (Ibn Daud, l.c.).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Talmud has numerous references to sages who were said to be descendants of King David or “from the House of David.” This is particularly noticeable in references to Hillel “the Elder” (circa 100 BCE), who is called a descendan
2 CONC t of Shephatiah, son of Abital (tractates Ketubot and Taanit).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sixteen generations of his descendants were called “Nasi” (prince) up to approximately 425 CE. The Talmud names only two or three generations of sages and is helpful in identification, but not in tracing genealogies.
2 CONT
2 CONT An important source is Iggeret Rav Sherira Gaon (The Letter of Rabbi Sherira Gaon), written in 986 CE. This was an early responsum by the head of the Talmudic Academy of Pumbedita in Babylonia. He was one of the dynasty of exilarc
2 CONC hs (Resh Galuta, or Prince of the Exiles), who claimed descent from King David.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second part of the Letter discusses the exilarchs and the heads of the academies in Sura and Pumbedita. Rabbi Sherira attempts to distinguish between facts and stories and presents an early post-biblical history written largel
2 CONC y in Aramaic. This Letter serves as a major source of the chronology and genealogy of the Geonim.
2 CONT
2 CONT The institutions of the exilarch began with the descendants of Jehoiachin, last King of Judah, who was exiled to Babylonia. It survived until 825 CE with Jews continuing to view the exilarch as their leader for several centuries a
2 CONC fter its decline.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sherira's important Letter brings the Davidic genealogy to his time. <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> "Babylonian academy head (gaon), Sherira ben Hanina came from a noble family
2 CONT
2 CONT which claimed to trace its ancestry to King David.
2 CONT
2 CONT Appointed gaon of the academy of Pumbedita in 968
2 CONT
2 CONT at the age of 62, he held the position almost until his death at the age of a hundred.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was considered the second to the last of the truly great gaonim,
2 CONT
2 CONT his son Hai Gaon being the last."
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- Sherira Gaon
2 CONT
2 CONT Sherira ben Chanina Yehuda Gaon (ca.906–1006) was a jurist descended from an exilarchic family who served as gaon of the Pumbedita yeshiva from 968 to 1004. He was the descendant, both on his father's and his mother's side, of pro
2 CONC minent families, several members of which had occupied the gaonate. One of his ancestors was Rabbah b. Abuha, who himself belonged to the family of the exilarch. Sherira boasted that his genealogy could be traced back to the pre-B
2 CONC ostanaian branch of that family, which he claimed, on account of the deterioration of the exilarchate had renounced its claims thereto (Banu Tzaluba), preferring instead the scholar's life (Letter of Sherira Gaon, in Neubauer, l.c
2 CONC . i. 23, 33). The seal of his family was a lion, which was said to have been the emblem of the Judean kings (Ibn Daud, l.c.).
2 CONT
2 CONT Directly prior to his appointment, the Pumbedita academy had been substantially weakened by a schism that led to the short-lived founding of a breakaway faction by Nehemiah bar Kohen Ṣedeq. In addition, throughout this period, the
2 CONC  Babylonian academies (see Yeshivot in Babylonia/Iraq) were losing much of their influence in the Jewish communities to the west thanks to the rise of houses of study (Heb. batte midrash) there.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the flow of financial contributions and legal inquiries to Baghdad on the wane, Sherira strove to strengthen his academy and revitalize these attenuated connections. Legal responsa, many of which survive, constituted his prim
2 CONC ary medium for such efforts. In 986, Sherira wrote an extended responsum on a series of questions addressed to him by Jacob ben Nissim ibn Shāhīn in Qayrawan concerning the evolution of the corpus of talmudic literature and the hi
2 CONC story of the geonim (see Gaon & Gaonate). This responsum, nearly fifteen thousand words in length and often referred to as his “ Epistle” (Heb. ’Iggeret), has been preserved in two recensions, each of which addresses different asp
2 CONC ects of the oral and written redaction of the Talmud. The Epistle itself describes the history of the two competing academies of Babylonia and presents a chronology of their leaders. Much of what is says has been corroborated by i
2 CONC ndependent documentary evidence, making the Epistle one of the most important works of medieval Jewish historiography. Sherira displays a clear preference for the Pumbeditan academy, portraying it as the sole surviving link with t
2 CONC he glorious heritage of Babylonian Jewry, particularly since the Sura yeshiva was closed during much of the tenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT He describes the nature and composition of the Mishnah, the Tosefta, the baraitot, the Talmud, and the history of the talmudic sages (amoraim and saboraim) and the geonim up to his own time. The main questions he answers are: how 
2 CONC and why were these works written, and who were the heads of the academies and scholars who continued the Shalshelet ha-Qabbalah (the chain of tradition) after the saboraim?
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the many scholars who have explicitly used the Iggeret are Menahem ben Abraham ibn Zerah, Profiat Duran, and Abraham ben Samuel Zakuto. Modern scholarship is divided over the possible influence of the Iggeret on Maimonides' 
2 CONC work and on Abraham ibn Daud's Sefer ha-Qabbalah. For modern research, the Iggeret has served as a primary source for the redactional history of the Mishnah and the chronology of the talmudic period. Although the Iggeret was writt
2 CONC en as a letter and in the style of answers to concrete questions, Sherira has traditionally been regarded as "the first Jewish 'historian' of the medieval period" (N. Roth).
2 CONT
2 CONT Esteem for Sherira’s writings maintained the status of the Babylonian academies for a time, and played a key role in making the Babylonian Talmud the supreme source of Jewish legal authority. In addition to historiography and talm
2 CONC udic commentary, Sherira also took an interest in mysticism. Contrary to a talmudic ruling, he permitted the practice of addressing prayers to angels in Aramaic. Sherira assumed the headship of the Pumbeditan academy when he was m
2 CONC ore then sixty years old. In 1004, somewhat enfeebled, he abdicated in favor of his son Hay Gaon. News of his abdication spread slowly, and inquiries continued to be addressed jointly to Sherira and his son, who had been chief jud
2 CONC ge (Heb. av bet din) of the academy for some time.
2 CONT
