1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jonah (Abū ʾl-Walīd) ibn Ḥasday ha-Levi was a grammarian and poet in Lucena from the end of the tenth century through the early part of the eleventh. We know of him from later quotations. According to Moses ibn Ezr
2 CONC a (Kitāb	 al-Muḥāḍara, p. 31v), Ibn Ḥasday, to whom he gives the title rāʾīs (nasi), lived and worked as a teacher in Lucena between the second and third generations, alongside Abū Zakariyya ben Ḥanīga and Abū ʿAmr (Abraham) ibn Y
2 CONC aqwā—that is, immediately after, or at the same time as, the disciples of Menahem ibn Sarūq and Dunash ben Labraṭ, and before grammarians like Jonah ibn Janāḥ, Isaac ibn Yashūsh, and Samuel ibn Naghrella entered the scene. Moses i
2 CONC bn Ezra places him in the generation of the Lucena poets Abū Ibrahim ibn Barūn and Abū Sulayman Ibn Rashlā.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonah ibn Janāḥ (Rikma, p. 175) considered Ibn Ḥasday one of the most distinguished teachers of the Hebrew language. He cites Ibn Ḥasday’s opinion that it was possible to defend the existence of a supposed root, nqḥ, used to conju
2 CONC gate the verb laqaḥ (take) in the imperfect (halakh-yelekh type) because the Hebrew language does not usually assimilate the letter lamed, but rather nun (despite the fact that Ḥayyūj himself defended the existence of this particu
2 CONC lar assimilation). Jonah ibn Janāḥ alludes to a long conversation on this matter with his teacher Ibn Ḥasday. He also includes in one of his tracts (p. 317) another interpretation of the imperatives of weak second radical verbs. A
2 CONC braham ibn Ezra, in his Sefer	 Moznayim (p. 68*), presents Ibn Ḥasday’s theory on the value of the dagesh which indicates assimilation in the flexion of the verbs, but he does not allude to any specific treatise by Ibn Ḥasday.
2 CONT
2 CONT It seems certain that Abū ʾl-Walīd Jonah ibn Ḥasday and Abū ʿAmr Joseph ibn Ḥasday were brothers. Almost nothing else is known about this author and his work.
2 CONT
2 CONT José Martínez Delgado
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Ibn Ezra, Abraham. Sefer Moznayim, intr. and ed. L. Jiménez Patón and A. Sáenz-Badillos (Cordova: El Almendro, 2002).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ibn Ezra, Moses. Sefer	 ha-ʿIyyunim ve-ha-Diyyunim: ʿAl ha-Shira ha-ʿIvrit (Kitāb al-Muḥāḍara wa ʾl-Mudhākara), ed. A. S. Halkin (Jerusalem: Hoṣaʾat Meqiṣe Nirdamim, 1975).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ibn Janāḥ, Jonah. Opuscules et traités d’Abou 'l-Walid Merwan ibn Djanah de Cordoue, ed. Joseph Derenbourg and Hartwig Derenbourg (Paris: Imprimerie Nationale, 1880).
2 CONT
2 CONT ———. Sefer ha-Riqma = Kitāb al-luma. Ab Jonah Ibn Ganah lingua Arabica conscriptus, hebraica vertit Jehudah ibn Tibbon, ed. Michael Wilensky, publ. David Tené (Jerusalem: Academy of the Hebrew Language, 1976).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sáenz-Badillos, Angel, and Judit Targarona. Diccionario	 de autores judíos (Sefarad. Siglos X–XV) (Cordova: El Almendro, 1988), p. 26.
