1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mar_Ukba '''Mar Ukba'''] Wikipedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mar Ukba ben Judah''' (Hebrew: מר עוקבא בן יהודה; early tenth century) was the Babylonian Exilarch from 890 up until his banishment in 917. He was briefly reinstated again in 918, however, the following year he was disposed and
2 CONC  succeeded by his contentious cousin, David ben Zakkai.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in the mid-ninth century in Baghdad, Iraq, his father Judah ben David was Exilarch from 840-857, and a fifth-generation descendent of Bostanai. Following his uncle, Zakkai ben Ahunai's death in 890, he succeeded Zakkai as Exi
2 CONC larch, rather than Zakkai's son David, who was deemed too controversial for the position. Mar Ukba's early years as Exilarch were relatively calm, however, following the appointment of Kohen Tzedek Kahana ben Joseph as Pumbedita G
2 CONC oan in 917, a violent controversy broke out between him and Mar Ukba, over the question of the income of the academy from the region of Khorasan. This ultimately resulted in a boycott by Kohen Tzedek's wealthy and influential frie
2 CONC nd, Joseph ben Phinehas, who convinced Abbasid Caliph Al-Muqtadir to dipose Mar Ukba, which he did in 917. Soon afterward, Mar Ukba moved to Kermanshah in the spring of 917. However, after the young Caliph moved to his summer pala
2 CONC ce in Safran, Mar Ukba devised a scheme to win the royal favour by meeting Al-Muqtadir's secretary daily in his gardens and greeting him with the recitation of beautiful verses of poetry. These pleased the Caliphs secretary so muc
2 CONC h that he wrote them down and showed them to his master, who in his turn was so delighted that he sent for Mar Ukba. When the Caliph saw Mar Ukba, he asked him to express any wish. To this, Mar Ukba requested to be reinstated as E
2 CONC xilarch. The Caliph granted this wish, and soon after, Mar Ukba returned to Baghdad, where he was reinstated as Exilarch in 918. However, only a few months after his reinstatement, Kohen Ẓedeḳ and his friends once again succeeded 
2 CONC in securing his deposition and banishment from the country. Following this, Mar Ukba moved to Kairouan, Tunisia, where he was received with great esteem as a member of the House of David. In the Kairouan synagogue, a throne was bu
2 CONC ilt or him near the Torah ark, and he was always the third to read the weekly parashah. His immediate descendants are unknown, however, they probably immigrated to Francia. According to Jewish tradition, Yitskhak Eizik Meisels (b.
2 CONC  1425), the progenitor of the Meisels family was a 10th generation descendant of Mar Ukba.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT According to the [https://www.davidicdynasty.org/wp-content/uploads/2014/03/meizels.pdf Meisels family tree] made by davidicdynasty.org as well as traditional rabbinic literature (see: [https://beta.hebrewbooks.org/reader/reader.a
2 CONC spx?sfid=35411#p=480&fitMode=fitwidth&hlts=&ocr= "Sefer Nezer HaChaim", Vyzhnytsia, 1996]), Rabbi Yitskhak Eizik Meisels of Prague (b. 1425) was a 10th generation paternal descendant of the second to last Exilarch, Mar Ukba (b. 86
2 CONC 0). Both of those figures' birth years are based on very rough estimates so give or take 30 years or so, that is a 500 gap between Rav Meisels of Prague and Mar Ukba. However, assuming that each generation is about *25 years apart
2 CONC , that would mean that 10 generations between these two figures would only span some 250 years as opposed to 500. This means one of two things:
2 CONT # Mar Ukba had a male descendant line who continuously had children in their old age, up until Rabbi Yitskhak Eizik Meisels of Prague
2 CONT # The traditional narrative of 10 generations needs to be reexamined.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Then, of course, there is the problem of genealogical fabrications, however, the main goal of this profile is only to represent the traditional Jewish narrative.  
