1 NAME Yehudah "Judah" /ben Mevorakh/ Nagid, Raʾīs al-Yahūd al-Fustat
2 GIVN Yehudah "Judah"
2 SURN ben Mevorakh
2 NSFX Nagid, Raʾīs al-Yahūd al-Fustat
2 _MARNM ben Sa'adya
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1020
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1080
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Judah ben Saʿadya was the eldest of the five sons of Saʿadya ben Mevorakh. A physician at the Fatimid court like his father, Judah appears in Cairo Geniza records for the first time in 1043. He initially had two ti
2 CONC tles,rosh kalla (head of the assembly), given him by one of the leaders in Iraq after the closing of the Sura and Pumbedita academies, and he-ḥaver ha-meʿulle (exalted member) of the Jerusalem yeshiva. Sometime between late 1062 a
2 CONC nd mid-1064, however, he became the first Egyptian to bear the title nagid , probably given him by the gaon of Jerusalem, Elijah ha-Kohen ben Solomon (1062–1083). The gaon apparently extended this honor to Judah to keep him and th
2 CONC e Jews of Egypt loyal to the Jerusalem gaonate; in doing so he confirmed Judah’s exercise of local power.
2 CONT
2 CONT At first Judah’s power was of the traditional sort exercised by a courtier who interceded on behalf of the Jewish community. But gaonic recognition set him on the path to a more official kind of power and ultimately led to the est
2 CONC ablishment of the office of head of the Jews (raʾīs al-yahūd) in Egypt, a post whose prerogatives his brother Mevorakh ben Saʿadya and his nephew Moses ben Mevorakh greatly expanded. During Judah’s tenure, the office remained quas
2 CONC i-formal and based on his personal prestige. He presided over a majlis (audience chamber) where he heard petitions, held court, and conducted other communal business, but the sources never refer to him as raʾīs al-yahūd. Nor do th
2 CONC ey suggest that he had received a Fatimid writ of appointment, as some of his successors would, although this may only reflect administrative conditions at the Fatimid court during the anarchical 1060s and 1070s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judah ben Saʿadya died between 1077 and 1079 (Cohen records the date as ca. 1078), and appears in the Geniza for the last time in a poem by Solomon ha-Kohen ben Joseph Av about the defeat of the Seljuks at Cairo in 1077. The poem 
2 CONC mentions him as a high-ranking courtier (zeqan ha-hod, elder of splendor) in the entourage of the Fatimid vizier Badr al-Jamālī. Judah’s brother, Mevorakh, continued the relationship with the vizier, serving as his personal physic
2 CONC ian, and was ultimately reinstated in office with the help of Badr al-Jamālī’s son al-Afḍal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marina Rustow
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Cohen, Mark R. Jewish Self-Government in Medieval Egypt: The Origins of the Office of Head of the Jews, ca. 1065–1126 (Princeton, N.J.: Princeton University Press, 1980).
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation Marina Rustow. " Judah ben Saʿadya." Encyclopedia of Jews in the Islamic World. Executive Editor Norman A. Stillman. Brill Online , 2013. Reference. Jim Harlow. 15 January 2013 <http://referenceworks.brillonline.com/entri
2 CONC es/encyclopedia-of-jews-in-the-islamic-world/judah-ben-saadya-SIM_000406>
