1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Nethanel ben Mevorakh
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicknamed Abu al-Barakāt, Nethanel ben Mevorakh was the middle son of Mevorakh ben Saʿadya, who served as ra'īs (or colloquially rayyis) al-yahūd (Ar. head of the Jewish community, i.e. nagid) of Fustat from ca. 1078 to 1082 and f
2 CONC rom 1094 to 1111. Nethanel seems to have been born around 1095. Unlike his father and his brother Moses, Nethanel did not ascend to the headship. On the other hand, he was an active participant in the culture of the political and 
2 CONC economic elite of his community, and may well have served the Fatimid court, as perhaps is indicated by allusions to him in Geniza documents that style him sar ha-sarim (Heb. prince of princes) and beḥir ha-yeshiva (Heb. chosen on
2 CONC e of the academy). He may also have been learned, as both a eulogy dedicated to his mother and another letter refer to him as ḥakham (Heb. sage).
2 CONT
2 CONT Phillip Ackerman-Lieberman
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Cohen, Mark R. Jewish Self-Government in Medieval Egypt (Princeton, N.J.: Princeton University Press, 1980).
2 CONT
