1 NOTE {geni:about_me} According to the Telmud Yerushalmi, Rab Abba bar Aibu was a descendant of Shimei, brother of King David (Kes. 62b) or Shephatai, the son of Avital and King David (TY Taan. 4:2, 68a)
2 CONT
2 CONT Rab Abba Arika ben Aivu of Babylon was head of the rabbinic Academy between 210 and 250 in Sura, Babylonia. He was a Babylonian disciple of Judah the Prince who began to propagate the Mishna and related tannaitic literature as the
2 CONC  yardsticks of normative practice. He began Babylonian Gemara. He was a co-founder of the Babylonian Talmud.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rab travelled from Kafri, Babylon, to live with Rabbi Chyya in Eretz Israel as a youth in ordre to study.  Rabbi Chiyya taught him and guided him...and took him to visit Judah HaNasi.  Rabbi Chiyya explained some of the Nasi's cus
2 CONC toms to Rab; Rab later became a member of Rabbi Chiyya's Beit Din.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rab Abba Arika ben Aivu of Babylon was the nephew of Chiyya (bar Abba), another disciple of Judah ha Nasi, head of the school in Tiberias 180-230, and compiler of the Tosefta. On the Name of the Messiah, Rab (Abba Arika) said: --"
2 CONC The world would not have been created except for David." On the The Kingdom of Heaven , Rabbi Judah (bar Ezekiel) said in the name of Rab (Abba Arika): -- "If one is saying the Shema when he is walking, he should stop to receive t
2 CONC he kingdom of Heaven while standing. And what portion is called "the Kingdom of Heaven"? The LORD our God, the LORD is One!" (Deut 6:4b). --- Midrash, Siphre 'al Debarim 29.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gemara compiled at Sura---which refers to Abba Arika simply as Rab ["the Master"]--- was eventually expanded to include the rabbinic debates at rival Mesopotamian schools [especially Nehardea & Pum Beditha]. This inclusion of 
2 CONC rival viewpoints gives the Babylonian Gemara a distinctive dialectical characteristic. On Living with God, a familiar (word) in the mouth of Rab (Abba Arika): --"In the world to come there is no eating and drinking, no propagating
2 CONC  or multiplying, no bearing or giving / no envy, no hatred and no strife. But the righteous rest, wearing crowns on their heads, and share in the glory of the Presence [Shekinah], of which it is said: -- "And they saw God and ate 
2 CONC and drank" (Exod 24:2). --- Babylonian Talmud, Berakoth 17a.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rab Abba Arika ben Aivu of Babylon returned to his native Mesopotamia to found a rabbinic Academy in 219 at Sura, Babylonia. He died in 247 at age 87 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Hebrew Sûrâ ). City in southern Babylonia on the Euphrates. According to Gaonic tradition ( Gaon ), in AD 219 after his years of study in Palestine (Palaestina) the Rabbinic scholar Abbâ Arîḵâ, commonly called Raḇ, established a 
2 CONC kind of Jewish house of instruction there, in which he assembled a circle of pupils for the purpose of studying the Torah. With interruptions, even after the death of Rab in 254, the city remained - alongside and in competition wi
2 CONC th Pumbedita - a religious and cultural centre of Babylonian Judaism until the 10th cent. AD.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ego, Beate (Osnabrück)
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT e. bashan, s. v. S., Encyclopaedia Judaica 15, 1972, 521-523 
2 CONT
2 CONT j. neusner, A History of the Jews in Babylonia, vol. 2, 1966, 232-234.
