1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Bādīs abū mannad nāṣir al-dawla, son and successor of al-Manṣūr, a prince of the Zīrid dynasty, succeeded his father on the 3. Rabīʿ I. 386 (26 March 996), as governor of Ifrīḳīya and Central Mag̲h̲rib. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His accession was confirmed by his suzerain al-Ḥākim bi-amriʾllāh the Fāṭimid Caliph of Egypt. He continued the war against the Zanāta and after entrusting the government of Tāhert (Tagdemt) to his uncle Ittūweft he sent against Z
2 CONC īrī b. ʿAṭya, sovereign of Fās, his other uncle Ḥammād who was defeated at Amsār. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bādīs then advanced in person and his adversary retired before him but while the former was occupied in the West, Falfūl b. Saʿīd, governor of Ṭobna, rebelled against him and with him Māksin and Zāwī the grand-uncles of Bādis, who
2 CONC m Bādīs had offended by his preferment of younger relatives in filling the offices of state. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Māksin and Zāwī were defeated by Ḥammād in 391 (1001), Zāwī fled to Spain where he founded the Zīrid dynasty of Granada. Meanwhile Bādīs had overtaken Falfūl, who, after besieging Bāg̲h̲āi (Bāg̲h̲aya) in vain,had turned his attent
2 CONC ion to Ḳairawān, and defeated him at Wādī Ag̲h̲lān (10 Ḏh̲u ’l-Ḳaʿda 389 = 22 Oct. 999). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Falfūl fled through the desert and found refuge in Tripoli where he died in 400 ¶ (1009-1010). Bādīs then marched against this town and received the submission of Warrū, brother and successor of Falful. While these events were tak
2 CONC ing place, Ḥammād founder of the Ḳalʿat of the Banū Ḥammād, had rebelled in anger at being deprived of the governorships of Tid̲j̲is and Constautine. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bādīs put himself at the head of an expedition against him and defeated him on the borders of the S̲h̲alif, whereupon Ḥammād having lost army and treasure, succeeded in fleeing to the fortress (al-Ḳalʿat) which he had built. He wa
2 CONC s saved by the death of Bādīs which took place in the night of the 29th or 30th of Ḏh̲u ’l-Ḳaʿda 406 (9th or 10th May 1016).
2 CONT
2 CONT Badis had a Jewish friend, ''Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm Ibn ʿAṭāʾ'' ('''Abraham ben Nathan''') was leader of Qayrawanese Jewry in the first third of the eleventh century. He was a member of a wealthy elite that included the Ben Berekhiah, 
2 CONC Tahertī, and Ibn al-Majjānī families. His father, Nathan, may have been a communal official, although this is not clear. He was a major supporter of the academy (bet midrash) in Qayrawan and was also a generous contributor to the 
2 CONC Babylonian yeshivot, particularly to the Sura yeshiva, the renewal of which he helped to finance. Ibn ʿAṭāʾ served as court physician to the Zirid amirs Bādis (r. 996–1016) and al-Muʿizz (r. 1016–1062) in al-Mahdiyya. Due to his a
2 CONC ccess to the ruler, Abraham ben Nathan bore the honorific title ''rosh kol ha-qehillot'' (Heb. head of all the congregations). In late 1015, Ibn ʿAṭāʾ received the title of negid ha-gola (Heb. nagid of the exile) from Hay Gaon of 
2 CONC the Pumbedita yeshiva. He was the first Diaspora leader to hold this exalted title. According to a letter in the Cairo Geniza (Bodl MS Heb. D 65, f.9), he was on a military campaign with Bādis when word of the title arrived. http:
2 CONC //www.geni.com/people/Abu-Is%E1%B8%A5%C4%81q-Ibrahim-Sahl-ibn-al-Nag-hd%C4%ABlah-ibn-Ata-al-yah%C5%ABd%C4%AB/6000000014632605003
2 CONT
2 CONT (René Basset)
