1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Jacob ben Makhir Ibn Tabin''' (also known as Prophatius; 1236–1312), grandson of Samuel, was a distinguished Jewish physician who worked in the medical faculty of the University of Montpellier. Jacob translated 
2 CONC many Arabic versions of Greek works into Hebrew, such as Euclid's Elements and Ptolemy's Almagest, as well as certain significant Arabic works by al-Ġazzālī and others. His own Quadrant contains a table of eleven fixed stars which
2 CONC  are to be used in the construction of the quadrant instrument of his own invention. Jacob also wrote a book of astronomical tables giving the ascensions of certain stars at Paris, and an Almanach perpetuum.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob ben Machir was also known as Don Profeit/Profiat Tibbon and Profatius Judaeus. He spent his youth with his relatives in Montpellier (Provence), where he surely learned Arabic. In the context of the polemics between Maimonide
2 CONC an and anti-Maimonidean thinkers, he helped to prevent the imposition in Montpellier of the law forbidding the study of philosophy to those below a certain age. He lived most of his life in Lunel, where he did most of his translat
2 CONC ions and probably practiced medicine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob's main interest seems to have been astronomy and astronomical instruments. His new quadrant (quadrans novus, also known as quadrans Judaicus) incorporated in a quarter circle all the applications of the classic astrolabe. Hi
2 CONC s astronomical works were influential during the Renaissance and were referred to by scientists such as Copernicus and Kepler. Most of his translations are still in manuscript.
2 CONT
2 CONT Writings in Hebrew
2 CONT
2 CONT Rova Israel; Almanach perpetuum (radix 1300-1301)
2 CONT
2 CONT Translations (Arabic to Hebrew)
2 CONT
2 CONT Averroes (?), Artis logicae compendium (1289); Averroes, Epitome historiae animalium Aristotelis (1302-1303); Al-Ghazālī, Speculationis trutinam; Euclid, Elementa (1256); Qusṭā ibn Lūqā, Liber operationis sphaerae (1256); Ḥunayn I
2 CONC bn Isḥaq, Euclid's Data (1272); Thābit ben Qurra's version of Autolykos of Pitane (?), De motu sphaera (1273); Isḥaq ben Ḥunayn, Sphericorum Menelai Alexandrini;Ibn al-Haytham, Opus astronomicum (1271); Ibn al-Saffār, Explicatio a
2 CONC strolabii;Jābir ibn Aflaḥ, Almagest compendium; Azarchiel, De tabula dicta çafiha (?)
2 CONT
2 CONT Translations (Arabic to Latin)
2 CONT
2 CONT Azarchiel, De astrolabio (with John of Brescia, 1263); De regimine sanitatis (1299); Azarchiel, De tabula dicta çafiha (with John of Brescia, 1263)
2 CONT
2 CONT Josefina Rodríguez Arribas
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Boffito, Giuseppi, and C. Melzi d'Eril. Almanach Dantis Aligherii sive Profacii Judaei Montispessulani (Florence: Olschki, 1908).
2 CONT
2 CONT ---. Il Quadrante d'Israel (Florence: Libreria Internazionale, 1922).
2 CONT
2 CONT Harper, Richard I. "Prophatius Judaeus and the Medieval Astronomical Tables," Isis 62.1 (1971): 61-68.
2 CONT
2 CONT Millás Vallicrosa, Jose María. Tractat de l'assafea d'Azarquiel (Barcelona: Facultat de filosofia i lletres, 1933).
2 CONT
2 CONT Steinschneider, Moritz. "Prophatii proemium in Almanach," Bulletino di Bibliografia e di Storia delle scienze mathematiche e fisiche 9 (1876): 595-614.
2 CONT
2 CONT C. Sirat, A History of Jewish Philosophy in the Middle Ages, 1985.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cite this page
2 CONT
2 CONT Josefina Rodríguez Arribas. "Ibn Tibbon, Jacob ben Machir." Encyclopedia of Jews in the Islamic World. Executive Editor Norman A. Stillman. Brill Online, 2013. <http://referenceworks.brillonline.com/entries/encyclopedia-of-jews-in
2 CONC -the-islamic-world/ibn-tibbon-jacob-ben-machir-SIM_0011190>
2 CONT
2 CONT "Ibn Tibbon." Religion Past and Present. Brill Online, 2013. Reference. Jim Harlow. 10 September 2013 <http://referenceworks.brillonline.com/entries/religion-past-and-present/ibn-tibbon-COM_10245>
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_ben_Machir_ibn_Tibbon  ''''Jacob ben Machir ibn Tibbon (Hebrew: יעקב בן מכיר ׳ן תיבון‎), of the Ibn Tibbon family, also known as Prophatius.
2 CONT
2 CONT Provençal, Jewish astronomer; born, probably at Marseilles, about 1236; died at Montpellier about 1304. He was a grandson of Samuel ben Judah ibn Tibbon. His Provençal name was Don Profiat Tibbon; the Latin writers called him Prof
2 CONC atius Judæus. Jacob occupies a considerable place in the history of astronomy in the Middle Ages. His works, translated into Latin, were quoted by Copernicus, Reinhold, and Clavius. He was also highly reputed as a physician, and, 
2 CONC according to Jean Astruc ("Mémoires pour Servir à l'Histoire de la Faculté de Médecine de Montpellier," p. 168), was regent of the faculty of medicine of Montpellier. In the controversy between the Maimonists and the anti-Maimonis
2 CONC ts Jacob defended science against the attacks of Abba Mari and his party; the energetic attitude of the community of Montpellier on that occasion was due to his influence. Jacob became known by a series of Hebrew translations of A
2 CONC rabic scientific and philosophical works, and above all by two original works on astronomy. His translations are:
2 CONT * 	1.	the Elements of Euclid, divided into fifteen chapters;
2 CONT * 	2.	the treatise of Qusta ibn Luqa on the armillary sphere, in sixty-five chapters;
2 CONT * 	3.	Sefer ha-Mattanot, the Data of Euclid;
2 CONT * 	4.	a treatise of Autolycus on the sphere in movement;
2 CONT * 	5.	three treatises on the sphere of Menelaus of Alexandria;
2 CONT * 	6.	Ma'amar bi-Tekunah, or Sefer 'al Tekunah, in forty-four chapters;
2 CONT * 	7.	a treatise on the use of the astrolabe
2 CONT * 	8.	compendium of the Almagest of Ptolemy
2 CONT * 	9.	Iggeret ha-Ma'aseh be-Luaḥ ha-Niḳra Sofiḥah,
2 CONT * 	10.	preface to Abraham bar Ḥiyya's astronomical work;
2 CONT * 	11.	an extract from the Almagest on the arc of a circle;
2 CONT * 	12.	"Ḳiẓẓur mi-Kol Meleket Higgayon," Averroes' compendium of the Organon (Riva di Trento, 1559);
2 CONT * 	13.	Averroes' paraphrase of books xi-xix of Aristotle's history of animals;
2 CONT * 	14.	Mozene ha-'Iyyunim, from Ghazali.
2 CONT
2 CONT The two original works of Jacob are: (1) a description of the astronomical instrument called the quadrant (Bibliothèque Nationale, Paris, MS. No. 1054), in sixteen chapters, the last of which shows how to construct this instrument
2 CONC . This was translated several times into Latin; (2) astronomical tables, beginning with March 1, 1300 (Munich MS. No. 343, 26). These tables were translated into Latin and enjoyed great repute.
