1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Una de las más notables manifestaciones de ese arte en Burgos fueron los enormes retablos del tipo historiado: inmensos planos cuadriculados, con escenas religiosas, de madera tallada y dorada. El acaudalado y piad
2 CONC oso burgalés quiso ago aún más suntuoso, y á su deseo surgió aquel retablo de San Nicolás, céle- bre ya en la historia artística de España, cuyas visión y análisis están en todos los ojos y en todos los libros. Labrólo en pie- dra
2 CONC , con valiente composición, de la que es centro la hermosa efigie del Santo titular, y motivo culminante el grupo de la Coro- nación de la Virgen, nimbado por inmenso círculo de coros angé-lieos, y de la que son comentarios las hi
2 CONC storias milagrosas de San Nicolás, variedad de escenas sagradas, y las efigies orantes de los donadores; todo encuadrado en prodigiosa serie de agujas, pinácu- los, doseletes, repisas, estatuitas y cardinas, esculpidas en el más f
2 CONC antástico estilo gótico florido. Y al pie, como en humilde ofren- da, están los sepulcros del fundador, Gonzalo López de Polanco, fallecido en 1505; de su mujer, Leonor de Miranda, en 1503; de su hermano Alfonso López de Polanco, 
2 CONC muerto en 1491, y de su esposa Constancia de Maluenda, en 1520.
2 CONT
2 CONT   San Nicolás de Bari, Burgos Language Watch Edit Iglesia de San Nicolás de Bari is a Catholic church on Fernán González street in Burgos, Spain, located next to the Camino de Santiago, behind the Cathedral of Burgos. It is mainly
2 CONC  known for having one of the largest altarpieces in Spain and the only one carved in limestone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Iglesia San Nicolas de Bari behind the Cathedral of Burgos Since the Church of San Esteban was turned into a museum, it has served as the seat of the parish of San Esteban.
2 CONT
2 CONT Main Altarpiece of the Church Description	Edit The church was built in 1408, replacing another Romanesque temple.[1] Inside the temple presides one of the most monumental altarpieces of the Castilian Renaissance art, designed and 
2 CONC produced in the fifteenth century by Simón de Colonia and his son Francisco de Colonia The altarpiece dedicated to St. Nicholas is an imposing retable that is unique among its contemporaries in terms of its size and its materials.
2 CONC  It is both the largest lay commissioned carved retable from late medieval Burgos and the only altarpiece made from stone that survives from the city's late medieval period
2 CONT
2 CONT This main altarpiece was financed by the Burgos merchant Don Gonzalo Lopez de Polanco prior to his death in 1505. The work measures 15.55 by 9.04 meters and is made of a porous limestone from Hontoria de Cantera, a small town in t
2 CONC he province of Burgos. This type of stone was often used in the construction of buildings, including the Cathedral of Burgos and the Monasteries of San Pablo but was seldom used for altarpieces.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The high altarpieces from the Cathedral of Toledo (c. 1498-1504), the Cathedral of Seville (c. 1482-1492), the Carthusian Monastery of El Paular (c. 1490-1500), and the Cathedral of Oviedo (c. 1500-1505), are all of similar in sca
2 CONC le to the San Nicolas de Bari's altarpiece but the majority of these works were carved from wood and then polychromed.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to its Gothic tombs, the Renaissance arch of María Sáez de Oña and Fernando de Mena and the tables of the Burgos School of the Master of San Nicolás are also of great interest. Many of the works of art were also paid f
2 CONC or by the Polanco family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Church of San Nicolas de Bari in Burgos was declared a National Monument in Spain on January 26, 1917.
2 CONT
2 CONT References	Edit ^ Ríos, Rodrigo Amador de los (1888). Burgos (in Spanish). D. Cortezo y Ca. p. 624. ^ Reineck, Emily. "Piety and the Merchant Patron: A Case study of merchant patronage in early sixteenth-century Burgos (p. 8-9)" (
2 CONC PDF). ^ Reineck, Emily. "Piety and the Merchant Patron: A Case study of merchant patronage in early sixteenth-century Burgos (p. 17)" (PDF).
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________________
