2 CONT Gamaliel the Elder /ɡəˈmeɪljəl/,[1] or Rabban Gamaliel I (רבן גמליאל הזקן; Greek: Γαμαλιήλ ο Πρεσβύτερος), was a leading authority in the Sanhedrin in the mid-1st century CE. He was son of Simeon ben Hillel, and grandson of the gr
2 CONC eat Jewish teacher Hillel the Elder, and died twenty years before the destruction of the Second Temple in Jerusalem (70 CE). He fathered a son, whom he called Simeon, after his father, and a '''daughter, whose daughter (i.e., Gama
2 CONC liel's granddaughter) married a priest named Simon ben Nathanael.''' The name Gamaliel is the Greek form of the Hebrew name meaning reward of God.
2 CONT
2 CONT In another tradition, '''Shimon ben Nethanel married the daughter of Gamliel HaZaken.'''
