1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Provençal scholar; lived at the end of the fourteenth century and at the beginning of the fifteenth. He was a disciple of Frat Maimon, under whose direction he composed in 1424 his first work, a commentary on the "
2 CONC Cuzari." This commentary, still extant in manuscript (Bibliothèque Nationale, Paris, MS. No. 677, and elsewhere), is based upon the Hebrew translation of the "Cuzari" made by Judah Cardinal. Caspi was also the author of the follow
2 CONC ing works: (1) a commentary on the "Ruaḥ Ḥen," which treats of the terminology of Maimonides (ib. No. 678, 3; Parma, No. 395); (2) a commentary on Maimonides' "Shemoneh Peraḳim" (Paris, No. 678; Parma, No. 395); (3) "Liḳḳutot," a 
2 CONC collection of glosses on the Pentateuch (Munich MS. No. 252). These glosses are based upon those of Joseph Official. Many rabbis of eastern France are cited in these glosses, and many French words and sentences may be found in the
2 CONC m.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography: Zeit. für Hebr. Bibl. xvi. 128 et seq.; Berliner, Peletat Soferim, pp. 31 et seq.; Neubauer, Cat. Bodl. Hebr. MSS. col. 434; Renan-Neubauer, Les Ecrivains Juifs Français, pp. 755 et seq.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/4109-caspi-nathanael-ben-nehemiah
