1 OCCU Lieutenant governor of all of New Spain and Factor of the Royal Treasuary in Mexico, Gobernador y Factor de Nueva España
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gonzalo de Salazar was the royal factor or business manager for the treasury in New Spain. He came to New Spain in 1524 on the same ship as Cristóbal de Oñate.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gonzalo de Salazar (b. Granada, Spain; d. ca. 1564, New Spain) was leader of several councils that governed New Spain while Hernán Cortés was traveling to Honduras, in 1525-26.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gonzalo de Salazar was the first Christian child born in Granada after the conquest against the Moors (1492). Consequently, he was granted titles, special privileges, and at an early age, appointed royal page to the Catholic Monar
2 CONC chs at court in Granada. He fought in the Castilian War of the Communities, opposing the rebels against Emperor Charles V. For this, Charles rewarded him with the position of factor (tax collector), and Captain General (see below)
2 CONC  of New Spain from 29 December 1524, until 29 January 1526. Prior to the appointment of a Viceroy in New Spain, Gonzalo had worked, alongside Pedro Almindez Chirino, Alonso de Estrada (who preceded him, and succeeded him—they were
2 CONC  on bad terms), and others, and endured power struggles and controversy. Gonzalo's father, Doctor Guadalupe de Salazar, a converso, was royal physician, to the Catholic monarchs, and one of 16 "regidores" (administrators) of Grana
2 CONC da during the struggle to oust the Moors. Gonzalo, his wife Catalina, and her brother Antonio De La Cadena Maluenda (treasurer of New Spain), left Spain in 1524, and arrived in Mexico City in 1525. Gonzalo, became an encomendero (
2 CONC holder of an encomienda) of Tajimaroa (Michoacán), and held lands, and titles elsewhere. His daughter Catalina de Salazar de la Cadena first married Ruy de Mendoza, of the famous Mendoza clan, and subsequently wed Cristobal de Ona
2 CONC te. Their son, Juan de Onate, established San Juan de Los Caballeros near present-day Santa Fe, New Mexico in 1598. Gonzalo's son Juan Velazquez de Salazar, inherited his estates. Gonzalo's had a brother named Juan Salazar Velazqu
2 CONC ez. Gonzalo Salazar's wife, Catarina (aka Catalina) de la Cadena Maluenda, was descended from Mossen Truchas de Calatayud* (*Reference: "El Libro Verde de Aragon"). The assumed the name Maluenda after the town where they lived. Th
2 CONC ey were money lenders to kings, and prominent spice, and silk merchants in Europe for hundreds of years. Pedro de Maluenda was commissary for Hernan Cortez; witnessed the destruction of the Aztec nation, but died of a fever 6 mont
2 CONC hs after the conquest. The Spanish colonial families were inter-related, of Christian, Sephardic (converso), or Jewish decent. Catalina and Antonio De La Cadena are descendants of Don Lorenzo Suarez de Figueroa 1345-1409, Master o
2 CONC f the Order of Santiago (St. James), and his second wife Maria Catalina de Orozco. This line is linked to most of Spanish and European nobility today. The Maluenda Palace, (aka Palace of the Count of Castifale), a mid-fifteenth ce
2 CONC ntury home, is situated next to Burgos Cathederal. Lorenzo Suarez de Figueroa (a Mendoza, he used his maternal surname) was Count (later Duke) of Feria. The Figueroa Palace of Feria, now a parador, is in Zafra, Badajoz, Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1524 Governor and Captain General Cortés left Mexico City for Honduras. He put the government in charge of Alonso de Estrada, royal treasurer of the colony appointed by Charles, Rodrigo de Albornoz and Licenciado Alonso de Zuaz
2 CONC o, with Estrada at the head. The transfer of power occurred October 12, 1524.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Cortés left Mexico City he was accompanied by Gonzalo de Salazar and Pedro Almíndez Chirino as far as Coatzacoalcos. Salazar and Almíndez used this opportunity to convince the conqueror that they should be included in the gov
2 CONC ernment. Cortés sent them back with two decrees. The first decree directed that they join the already-formed government of Estrada, Albornoz and Zuazo as its fourth and fifth members, provided that the two groups could reconcile t
2 CONC heir differences. The second decree directed that Salazar and Almíndez replace Estrada and Albornoz.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Salazar and Almíndez arrived back in the capital, they suppressed the first of these decrees, and made known only the second one, thus taking over the government. This took place on December 29, 1524. However, they made the m
2 CONC istake of admitting the deception to some friends. This resulted in a scandal, and on February 17, 1525, Estrada and Albornoz were admitted to the government, which now included all five men mentioned by Cortés. In order of import
2 CONC ance, these were Salazar (tax collector), Almíndez (inspector), Estrada (treasurer), Albornoz (accountant) and Zuazo (justicia mayor).
2 CONT
2 CONT The expanded governing council was the work of Zuazo, acting as an arbitrator based on the first decree received from Cortés. The two factions, however, were not really reconciled. Estrada and Albornoz objected to the arrangement.
2 CONC  On April 20, 1525, Salazar and Almíndez proclaimed that no officials were to recognize the authority of Estrada and Albornoz, on pain of 100 lashes and confiscation of property. This proclamation was signed by Zuazo, Cervantes, d
2 CONC e la Torre, Sotomayor, Rodrigo de Paz (a member of the ayuntamiento, or city government), and the clerk Pérez. Estrada and Albornoz left Mexico City to seek out Cortés and present their case to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT The council continued with three members (Zuazo was still a member) until April 20, 1525. In the middle of the night, Zuazo was arrested in his home and taken under guard to be sent back to Spain. He went as far as Santo Domingo, 
2 CONC where he spent the rest of his life, and died in 1527.
2 CONT
2 CONT In early 1526 a messenger (Martín de Orantes AKA Dorantes) from Cortés arrived in Mexico City. His instructions were to replace Salazar and Almíndez in the governing triumvirate with Francisco de las Casas and Pedro de Alvarado. I
2 CONC n the absence, or incapacity of Las Casas and Alvarado, Estrada and Albornoz were named as replacements. Orantes entered the city in secret and made contact with members of the opposition.
2 CONT
2 CONT At daybreak on January 28, 1526 Orantes, and other opponents of Salazar, and Almíndez left the convent where they were staying; marched along the streets shouting "Viva Cortés". They arrested Salazar, and succeeded in getting the 
2 CONC ayuntamiento to execute the orders of Cortés. Salazar was caged in public display in the Zocalo, but because of his powerful connections in Europe, and New Spain, was freed. Thereafter he behaved as if nothing has happened. Dubbed
2 CONC  "el gordo" behind his back, he was overly courteous, and courtly. Estrada and Albornoz rejoined the government, as Las Casas and Alvarado were absent. Almíndez was arrested later in Tlaxcala, and brought back to Mexico City. Estr
2 CONC ada and Albornoz governed from January 29, 1526 to June 24, 1526, or just short of six months. Thereafter, Cortés returned to his post (very briefly) on June 25, 1526. Salazar and Almíndez did not.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Afterwards
2 CONT
2 CONT Gonzalo Salazar returned to Spain, to the Spanish court, and was reappointed, and completed his position as factor in New Spain. He  returned to the colony in 1540, and lived comfortably until his death probably in 1564.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Allmand, Christopher and McKitterick, Rosamond, "The New Cambridge Medieval History, c. 1415-1500," Vol. 7, p. 155, published 1998.
2 CONT
