2 CONC pestry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Gedaliah was a physician, scholar, writer on Hebrew literature, and teacher, rabbi in Salonika and Adrianople. Father of three sons, 1) Yakob and , 2) Moshe and 3) '''Shmuel (a/k/a Samuel Moises Castiel''' of Cochin who was or
2 CONC iginally living in Cairo when hired by Afonso – a distant cousin through "Paloma" the courtesan of  Don Fadrique)  Samuel had a brother named Isaac Pinto Castiel.  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Josef Wolff in his “''Researches and missionary labours among the Jews, Mohammedans, and other sects''” Samuel Castiel was also called “The Prince” and “Governor” of Cochin Jewish Community, India.  Samuel was a trans
2 CONC lator and Physician for the local King of Cranganore. as well as translator and physician of Afonso de Albuquerque, the Second Governor of the Portuguese territiories in India.  Jewish translators were not converted to Christianit
2 CONC y since it was in the best interest of their employer that their employee was not ostracized by their co-religionists.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Castiel was a “White Jew” to whom the “black Jews” paid annual tribute – payments to the “Kings of Cochin” who had very strong ties to the Dutch East India Company.  This writer's research leads him to believe that Samuel C
2 CONC astile's Y-DNA Haplogroup is J2b2* since 37% of the Conchin Jewish Males share this haplogroup.  These very same Cochin “White Jews” were also referred to as Mudaliars.  Pardesi (foreigners) Jews known also as "White Jews" and Mal
2 CONC abar (or Black) Jews make allusion to the long history of tensions between them and the Pardesi Jews although they were a minority ,always managed to win a privileged position in relation to other Jews within the context of local 
2 CONC society.  Based on their influence and economic power , and occupying the high ground of purity of origins - fundamental aspect in the caste-based society - the “White” or “Paradesi Jews”, despite the fierce criticism of the rabbi
2 CONC s, managed to "push" the majority of Malabar Jews into an inferior social situation , at least until the advent of Mahatma Ghandi's liberation.  David B'laila, Samuel Castiel, Ephraim Salaḥ, and Joseph Levi built a synagogue in Cr
2 CONC anganore – 16 miles north of Cochin in 1568.
2 CONT
2 CONT The synagogue and community of Parur - one of the Malabar synagogue to be found in various places - is closely linked with the Portugues presence . It is the oldest of the remaining synagogues - the original building dated from 11
2 CONC 64 and was rebuilt in 1616 on the orders of David Jacob Castiel, the fourth Mudaliar according to the inscription on one of the walls.  This dates the arrival of “Castiel” family to roughly 1492.   According to an anonymous Hebrew
2 CONC  chronicle giving the history of the Malabar Jews, and now preserved in the University Library at Cambridge, England, his grandfather had come from Jerusalem; though this statement is perhaps not to be taken literally, as the whit
2 CONC eJews in Cochin are sometimes called "Jerusalem Jews" ("Jüd. Lit.-Blatt," xix. No. 25, p. 95; "Jewish Quarterly Review," i. 23). The same chronicle mentions a Ḥayyim Belilla (ḥazan), who had come from Safed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spanish and Portuguese exiles came after the Inquisition, and others arrived fleeing persecution in the Middle East. In 1560 the Portuguese set up an office of the Inquisition in Goa, halfway between Bombay and Cochin, and even mo
2 CONC re Jews sought the protection of Cheraman Parumal, the Raja of Cochin, referred to as "King of the Jews" by Portuguese authorities.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jews could not have survived under Portuguese rule (1502-1663) had it not been for Parumal. In 1565 he gave them a strip of land next to his palace and in 1568 permitted them to build a synagogue not 30 yards from his temple. 
2 CONC He appointed a hereditary mudaliar (chief) from among the Jews and invested the position with special privileges and jurisdiction in all internal matters in the Jewish community. This office continued in force under subsequent raj
2 CONC as and even under Dutch and British rule. The Hallegua (Halioua) family, which still holds the title, continues to be influential in Cochin.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are few places in the world where Jews can claim centuries of peaceful coexistence with the local population.  The district of Jew town in Cochin on the Malabar Coast of Southern India is one of them .
2 CONT
2 CONT Gedaliah ben Jacob Tam ibn Yaḥya (d. 1575), born into a distinguished Sephardi family of rabbis, intellectuals, and literati that originated in Spain, was one of the leading rabbis of Salonica during the second half of the sixteen
2 CONC th century. His father, Rabbi Jacob Tam ben David ibn Yaḥya (ca. 1475–1542) was a notable rabbi and intellectual, and the author of a book of responsa entitled Sheʾelot u-Teshuvot Ohale Tam (Responsa Tents of Uprightness). Both Ge
2 CONC daliah and his brother Joseph (d. 1534) studied medicine, Joseph apparently becoming one of the personal physicians of Sultan Suleiman the Magnificent (r. 1520–1566). His friend and fellow physician Amatus Lusitanus dedicated his 
2 CONC seventh Centuria Curationum (One Hundred Medical Cases) to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his youth, Gedaliah studied in the yeshivot of Salonica and Edirne, and returned to Istanbul in 1548. His wealth enabled him to act as a maecenas to scholars and also to assemble a large and important collection of printed book
2 CONC s and manuscripts. He was one of the city’s best-known poets during this period. According to Eliakim Carmoly(1802–1875) and some other scholars, he was a member of a “school of poetry” (ḥakhme ha-shir), a kind of academy for poet
2 CONC ry or association of Hebrew poets, but whether such a group actually existed requires further research. Gedaliah died in Istanbul in 1575 and was eulogized by Saʿadya ben Abraham Longo, who had enjoyed his patronage, in his book S
2 CONC eder Zemanim. At least two of Gedaliah’s sons, Jacob (d. 1596) and Moses (d. 1595), reached the age of maturity. Both of them were rabbis in Salonica: Jacob , in addition, was a notable poet and published many of his grandfather’s
2 CONC  works.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yaron Ben Naeh
2 CONT
