0 @N6000000003327798405@ NOTE
1 CONT G5.7. Deborah, married R. Naftali Hirsch Gunzburg as his first wife. He  was A.B.D. of Pinsk and Slutzk, and died in 1687. Deborah was from the second marriage. His first wife was Deborah's cousin, Rebecca, daughter of Judah Wahl 
1 CONC (G4.6. below). Deborah was also married to R. Jacob Weil, A.B.D.  Brest and Lublin, son of Ephraim Naftali Hirsch (Herz) Weil, A.B.D. Ludmir.  Herz's father was R. Jonah Weil with the title of Oberrabbiner of Burgau,  Schwaben and
1 CONC  Schweiz, with his seat in Gunzburg, son of R. Jacob Weil,  Landesrabbiner of Bavaria, son of the MaHaRaM Weil in Ulm, son of R. Jacob Weil (the prominent scholar known as MaHaRIY, died before 1456) of Nuremburg, son of Judah.
0 @N6000000000374551305@ NOTE BIOGRAPHY: Alguazil-mór do Santo Ofício da Inquisição
0 @N6000000007978010948@ NOTE BIOGRAPHY: Alguazil-mór do Santo Ofício da Inquisição
0 @N6000000005024494325@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Part I: Márquez-Mendoza
1 CONT
1 CONT Writing n 1954, Fray Angélico Chávez wrote the following in regard to the unidentified wife of Gerónimo Márquez, a soldier in the army of don Juan de Oñate: 
1 CONT
1 CONT His wife's name is not known, but an ambiguous statement makes her seem to be a Doña Ana de Mendoza, daughter and granddaughter of leading Conquistadores of New Spain. She had three sisters who were nuns, and was a niece of Don Fe
1 CONC rnando de Oñate as well as a first cousin of Francisco de Zaldívar. (ONMF: 71)
1 CONT
1 CONT The source of this information cited by Chávez was Archivo General de la Nacion (AGN), Mexico, Audiencia, legajo 72, Title 1489. In actuality the source from the AGN, Mexico, Audiencia is legajo 72, Title 148-148a, and consists of
1 CONC  two documents with twelve pages. A photostat of this document is available at the University of New Mexico's Zimmerman Library in the Southwest Reading Room. Undoubtedly, these documents had been consulted by individuals over the
1 CONC  decades since the publication of Chávez's Origins of New Mexico Families searching for clues to clear up the noted ambiguity. No progress was made until 1996 when Patricia Black Esterley of the New Mexico Genealogical Society pas
1 CONC sed on a photocopy of the two documents to myself in another attempt to clear the question at hand. Immediately there were a couple of seemingly difficult obstacles encountered with the documents: 1) the name of Gerónimo Márquez d
1 CONC oes not appear anywhere in the two documents, and 2) the person dictating the documents could not be easily identified.
1 CONT
1 CONT A diligent approach to reading the two documents revealed that the author of both documents was Doctor don Santiago del Riego, the husband of doña Ana de Mendoza, daughter of don Juan de Zaldívar y Oñate and doña Marina de Mendoza
1 CONC . The documents are addressed to the king of Spain from Riego, and provide information describing his merits and those of his wife, doña Ana. Riego's name is difficult to decipher, but is written three times, once on the cover of 
1 CONC the first document and then at the end of each of the two documents.
1 CONT
1 CONT The first document is dated February 24, 1597, Mexico City. Riego declared that he had served in the Real Audiencia of Nueva España for thirty-three years. Furthermore, he mentioned he had nine children by his wife, doña Ana de Me
1 CONC ndoza, whom he described as "hija y nieta de los Primeros y mas principales conquistadores desta nueva spana." It is apparently this statement that Chávez referred to when he wrote that doña Ana de Mendoza "was a daughter and gran
1 CONC ddaughter of leading Conquistadores of New Spain." The document is addressed to His Majesty from "El Dr Santiago del Riego."
1 CONT
1 CONT The second document contains two entries written to the king by don Santiago del Riego. The first entry is dated October 14, 1598, Mexico City, and contains the information from which Fray Angélico Chávez extracted material for hi
1 CONC s suggestive comments about the wife of Gerónimo Márquez. Riego declared he had been married with doña Ana de Mendoza for twenty-five years, and in addition to serving in the Real Audiencia de Nueva España and Nueva Galicia, he ha
1 CONC d been serving in the Real Audiencia de las Indias for the past nine years. Both of his wife's parents had died, leaving three minor daughters, each of who entered convents. This is the source of the reference made by Chávez that 
1 CONC doña Ana de Mendoza "had three sisters who were nuns." The next references by Chávez to doña Ana's uncle (tio), don Fernando de Oñate, and her first cousin (primo hermano), don Francisco de Zaldívar, came from the following statem
1 CONC ent found in the second entry made by don Santiago del Riego: ".como lo hizo don Frdo de Oñate su tio con quatro mil ps y Franco de zaldivar su primo hermano con tres mil. ." 
1 CONT
1 CONT There is no reference to Gerónimo Márquez in either entry of the second document. Rather, the entries relate to the foundation of the Convent of San Lorenzo in Mexico City established through doñations made by doña Ana de Mendoza,
1 CONC  her husband, and her relatives. Doña Ana and her husband provided 44,000 pesos for the founding of the convent. Her uncle, don Fernando de Oñate, gave 4,000 pesos, while her first cousin, Francisco de Zaldívar, contributed 3,000 
1 CONC pesos, with an additional 4,000 pesos given by another uncle, Cristóbal de Oñate.
1 CONT
1 CONT The information above confirms that doña Ana de Mendoza was not the wife of Gerónimo Márquez. The question of the identity of Márquez's wife remains unanswered. However, some clues as to her identity may lay in a brief reference f
1 CONC ound in Gaspar Pérez de Villagrá's epic account of the Oñate's colonization of New Mexico (Historia de la Nueva Mexico, 1610). Pérez de Villagra wrote, "The next post in order he gave/ To Captain Marcelo de Espinosa,/ With Gerónim
1 CONC o Márquez and Juan Díaz,/ Pedro Hernández and Francisco Márquez,/ These four all brothers. " (Canto XXVI).
1 CONT
1 CONT Did Pérez de Villagrá intend to state that these four men were siblings, or that they were related by marriage as brothers-in-law? Although it is probable that all four men were siblings using different family surnames it could ha
1 CONC ve been that Gerónimo Márquez and Francisco Márquez were brothers and Juan Diaz and Pedro Hernandez were brothers-in-law of Gerónimo and Francisco. Alternatively, these four men could have been four of the five sons that Gerónimo 
1 CONC Márquez brought with him to New Mexico, although Fray Angélico Chávez identified these sons as Francsico Márquez, Juan Márquez, Pedro Márquez, Hernando Márquez and Diego Márquez. Additional research is needed to clarify the relati
1 CONC onship between Gerónimo Márquez, Francisco Márquez, Juan Díaz and Pedro Hernández.
1 CONT
1 CONT A transcription of the document that refers to doña Ana de Mendoza, her sisters and famous uncles was published in Nuestra Raices (Journal of the Genealogical Society of Hispanic America), Vol. 8, No. 4, Winter 1996, 147-48.______
1 CONC _________________
1 CONT Gerónimo Márquez, one of Juan de Oñate's trusted captains, reveals some of the hardships of the times. In September 1598, Oñate ordered Márquez and four other soldiers to track down deserters from the colony who were headed back t
1 CONC o Mexico. They caught up to the runaways and executed two of them on the spot. Two months later, Márquez was part of a hurried retreat from the Acoma Pueblo after Indians killed the leader of his party and several other soldiers. 
1 CONC In January 1599, he returned as part of a force that killed and captured hundreds of Acomas in retaliation. In 1604-05, he was a member of an expedition led by Oñate to the Gulf of California; the party survived the return trip by
1 CONC  eating their horses. 
1 CONT
1 CONT About 1609, the Spanish colonists in New Mexico moved some 25 miles south from San Gabriel to a narrow valley that they thought would be easier to defend. They established a new settlement there, the city of Holy Faith, or Santa F
1 CONC é. Francisco de Madrid, Gerónimo Márquez, and others were among the original residents. Today, their tiny walled village is the oldest capital city in the United States.
0 @N6000000005024494919@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Captain of the artillery, native of San Luca la Mayor, 40 years of age, swarthy and black bearded, son of Hernan Martín Zambrano, with complete armor for himself and horse. He is married, has four sons and a daughter, and is takin
1 CONC g his whole household.
1 CONT ___________________
1 CONT Gerónimo Márquez was the Maestre de Campo (General) of the troops which joined Oñate in 1600. He was forty years old, a native of Sanlucar de Barrameda, the son of Hernán Muñoz Zamorano. He was described as swarthy and black-beard
1 CONC ed. In the Puana muster-roll of 1597 he had the rank of Capitán of Artillery, when his father's name was written as Hernán Martín Sambrano, and his birthplace as San Lucar la Mayor. With him came his wife and five grown sons. His 
1 CONC name runs through all the Oñate annals as an adventurous leader. He was exiled from New Mexico, but he returned-if ever the sentence was carried out. As late as 1631 he was living at his estancia at Acomilla in the Rio Abajo distr
1 CONC ict. 
1 CONT His wife's name is not  known....(see José Antonio Esquibel's Beyond Origins of New Mexico Families article below).
1 CONT Part I: MÁRQUEZ-Mendoza
